WEBVTT

00:00:00.102 --> 00:00:05.024
Ordinary equality and inequality comparisons can
produce UNKNOWN when null is involved

00:00:06.730 --> 00:00:12.414
PostgreSQL provides a useful alternative called IS
DISTINCT FROM You can think

00:00:12.427 --> 00:00:16.886
of this as a null-safe comparison IS DISTINCT FROM
tells us whether

00:00:16.912 --> 00:00:20.893
two values are different even when one of them is
null IS

00:00:20.919 --> 00:00:24.618
NOT DISTINCT FROM tells us whether two values match
including the case

00:00:24.631 --> 00:00:29.051
where both are null The useful part is that these
comparisons return

00:00:29.076 --> 00:00:34.883
TRUE or FALSE rather than UNKNOWN The SQL example
compares bill amount

00:00:34.896 --> 00:00:38.686
with amount paid Run it in the browser and inspect
the two

00:00:38.712 --> 00:00:44.232
comparison columns You can also copy the query into
pgAdmin and

00:00:44.258 --> 00:00:49.298
experiment with the values This operator becomes
very useful when the business

00:00:49.324 --> 00:00:52.056
rule needs missing values to be handled explicitly
